CAT = "cpctaCompliance"; SEG = "CMP"
# Domain 2 tasks 2A to 2Q; knowledge k65 to k84.
Q = [
("Which task is outside a patient care technician's usual scope of practice?",
 "Assessing a new admission", ["Taking vital signs", "Removing a peripheral IV", "Recording intake and output"],
 "Assessment and care planning belong to the registered nurse. Technicians collect data such as vital signs and report it, and may remove peripheral IVs where trained and allowed."),
("A patient asks the technician what his new lab results mean for his cancer. What is the best reply?",
 "\"I'll ask your nurse to explain.\"", ["\"They look bad to me.\"", "\"They look normal to me.\"", "\"I'll read them to you now.\""],
 "Explaining results and diagnoses is outside the technician's role. The technician passes the question to the nurse or provider."),
("A patient gives the technician a $50 tip for good care. What should she do?",
 "Politely decline the money", ["Keep it as a thank-you", "Split it with her coworkers", "Accept it if no one sees"],
 "Accepting cash gifts crosses professional boundaries and is against most facility policies. The technician thanks the patient and declines."),
("A patient's ID band was cut off for a procedure and not replaced. What is done before more care?",
 "A replacement band is applied", ["Use the room number instead", "Ask the patient his name only", "Tape the old band back on"],
 "Positive identification depends on checking two identifiers against the ID band. A missing band is replaced per policy before care continues."),
("An unconscious patient arrives after a car crash and needs emergency care. Which kind of consent applies?",
 "Implied consent", ["Written informed consent", "Verbal consent from staff", "No consent is ever needed"],
 "When a patient cannot consent and delay would cause harm, the law assumes the patient would agree to emergency care. This is implied consent."),
("An alert adult refuses his bath this morning. What does the technician do?",
 "Respect his refusal", ["Bathe him anyway as ordered", "Have two staff hold him still", "Skip it and tell no one"],
 "A competent patient has the right to refuse care. The technician may ask why and offer another time, then reports and documents the refusal."),
("Which action lowers a patient's fall risk in her room?",
 "Low bed, call light in reach", ["High bed with all rails up", "Room kept dark at night", "Wet floor left to dry"],
 "A low, locked bed, a call light and personal items within reach, nonslip footwear and good lighting all help prevent falls."),
("A confused patient keeps trying to climb out of bed. What does the technician do first?",
 "Stay with him and get help", ["Tie his wrists to the rails", "Stay and raise all four rails", "Give him a sleeping pill"],
 "Restraints need a provider's order and are a last resort; raising all four rails can be a restraint and a fall hazard. The technician stays with him for safety and gets the nurse."),
("A soft wrist restraint has been ordered and applied. How is it checked?",
 "Two fingers fit under it", ["It is tied to the side rail", "It is as tight as possible", "It is checked once a shift"],
 "A restraint should allow two fingers under it, be tied to the bed frame with a quick-release knot, and be checked and released often as policy requires."),
("A resident's caregiver leaves her in soiled clothes for hours and does not bring her meals. What type of abuse is this?",
 "Neglect", ["Financial abuse", "Sexual abuse", "Verbal abuse"],
 "Failing to give needed food, hygiene or care is neglect. Financial abuse is misuse of a person's money or property."),
("A child has bruises at different stages of healing and the parent's story keeps changing. What does the technician do?",
 "Report the concern", ["Ask the parent to explain again", "Wait to see if more bruises appear", "Tell the parent it was reported"],
 "Health care workers are mandated reporters of suspected abuse. The technician reports concerns through the facility's process; proof is not required to report."),
("Which department would usually help a patient who has no safe place to go after discharge?",
 "Social services", ["Dietary services", "Environmental services", "The pharmacy"],
 "Social workers help with discharge planning, housing, community resources and financial or family problems."),
("Which agency sets and enforces workplace safety rules for health care workers?",
 "OSHA", ["HIPAA", "CLIA", "HCAHPS"],
 "The Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) enforces workplace safety standards. NIOSH researches and recommends ways to prevent work injuries."),
("A technician pours a disinfectant from a large jug into a small spray bottle for the unit. Under OSHA's hazard communication rule, what must the spray bottle have?",
 "A label naming it and its hazards", ["A note of who filled it", "A color code by shift", "Nothing if it stays on the unit"],
 "A secondary container must be labeled with the product name and its hazards, unless it is for immediate use by the person who filled it. Unlabeled chemicals can be misused."),
("Under RACE, what does the C step ask staff to do?",
 "Close doors to contain it", ["Call the patient's family", "Clear the hallway of carts", "Check the fire extinguisher"],
 "RACE is Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish or Evacuate. Containing means closing doors and windows to slow the spread of fire and smoke."),
("When using a fire extinguisher, what does the A in PASS stand for?",
 "Aim at the fire's base", ["Alert the operator", "Activate the alarm", "Aim at the top of the flames"],
 "PASS is Pull the pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle, Sweep side to side. Aiming at the flames does not reach the fuel."),
("The technician hurts her back while helping move a patient. What should she do?",
 "Report it to her supervisor", ["Keep working and tell no one", "Wait to see if it gets worse", "Report it if pain lasts a week"],
 "Work injuries are reported to the supervisor right away and documented on an incident report, even if they seem minor."),
("Which item is protected health information under HIPAA?",
 "A name with a diagnosis", ["A hospital's public phone number", "A blank admission form", "A staff training schedule"],
 "PHI is health information that can identify a person, such as a name linked with a diagnosis or test result."),
("A coworker asks about a famous patient on another unit who is not in her care. What does the technician do?",
 "Decline to share details", ["Share only the diagnosis", "Look it up and tell her", "Share it if she is staff"],
 "Staff access and share PHI only when needed for their work. Curiosity is not a reason to view or discuss a patient's information."),
("Where is it acceptable to discuss a patient's condition with the care team?",
 "In a private area", ["In a crowded elevator", "In the cafeteria line", "At the nurses' station, loudly"],
 "Patient information is discussed only with people who need it, in places where it will not be overheard."),
("A patient asks to see his own medical record. What does HIPAA allow?",
 "He may see and copy it", ["He has no right to see it", "Only with a lawyer present", "Only after he is discharged"],
 "Patients have a right to access and get copies of their health records. The technician directs him to the nurse or health information department."),
("Under the Patient's Bill of Rights, which is a patient right?",
 "To refuse treatment", ["To see other patients' charts", "To pick a roommate", "To change their own orders"],
 "Patients have the right to information, privacy, respectful care and to refuse treatment after being told what may happen."),
("A technician makes an error in a paper chart entry. What is the correct fix?",
 "One line through it, initialed", ["Cover it with correction fluid", "Erase it completely", "Tear out the page"],
 "A single line keeps the original entry readable, followed by the correction, date and initials. Records are never erased or blotted out."),
("In the EHR, the technician charts a patient's vital signs. When should this be done?",
 "Soon after giving the care", ["At the end of the week", "Before taking the readings", "Only if the values are abnormal"],
 "Care is charted promptly after it is done. Charting before care is falsification, and normal values must be charted too."),
("What does the abbreviation ad lib mean on an activity order?",
 "As much as desired", ["Twice a day", "Nothing by mouth", "Before meals"],
 "Ad lib means as desired, so \"ambulate ad lib\" lets the patient walk as much as he likes. Before meals is ac and nothing by mouth is NPO."),
("What does the term dyspnea mean?",
 "Difficult breathing", ["Painful urination", "Fast heart rate", "Trouble swallowing food"],
 "Dys- means difficult and -pnea means breathing. Trouble swallowing is dysphagia; painful urination is dysuria."),
("A technician is unsure how to handle a patient problem and her nurse is busy. Who does she go to next?",
 "The charge nurse", ["The patient's family", "The hospital administrator", "A coworker on another unit"],
 "Following the chain of command means going to the next person in line, such as the charge nurse, rather than skipping levels or going outside the team."),
("A technician sees a nurse prepare to give medication without checking the patient's ID band. What is the appropriate action?",
 "Speak up to the nurse now", ["Tell the nurse after the shift", "Tell the patient to refuse", "File a report and say nothing"],
 "Every team member can speak up about a safety concern. Raising it at once, then using the chain of command if needed, protects the patient."),
("What does the HCAHPS survey measure?",
 "Patients' views of their care", ["Staff job satisfaction", "Lab test accuracy rates", "Infection rates by unit"],
 "HCAHPS is a national survey of patients' experience, such as how well staff communicated and responded to needs. Results are publicly reported."),
("What does a hospital's National Patient Safety Goal on alarms ask staff to do?",
 "Respond to alarms promptly", ["Turn off alarms that annoy", "Lower all alarm volumes", "Silence alarms promptly"],
 "The Joint Commission's goal on clinical alarm safety asks hospitals to make sure alarms are heard and answered. Silencing or ignoring alarms puts patients at risk."),
("A patient's call light does not light up when pressed. What does the technician do?",
 "Give another way to call and report it", ["Tell the patient to shout", "Check it again tomorrow", "Report it at the end of shift"],
 "The patient must always have a way to call for help, such as a hand bell, until the call light is fixed. The broken light is reported for repair right away."),
("Before helping a patient into a wheelchair, what does the technician do first?",
 "Lock the wheels", ["Unlock the wheels for movement", "Leave the footrests down", "Push it close but unlocked"],
 "Locked wheels keep the chair from rolling away during the transfer. The footrests are also swung out of the way so the patient does not trip."),
("A hostage or armed intruder situation is announced overhead. What does the technician do?",
 "Follow the emergency plan", ["Go to the area to help", "Leave the building at once", "Call the patient's family"],
 "Each facility has a plan and codes for violent or armed events. Staff follow it, protect patients where they are and do not go toward the threat."),
("A patient tells the technician she was rude yesterday. How does the technician respond?",
 "Listen and apologize", ["Explain she was very busy", "Say the patient is mistaken", "Ignore the comment"],
 "Listening and acknowledging the complaint without getting defensive is professional. The technician tells the nurse so the concern can be followed up."),
("A technician is running 10 minutes late for her shift. What is the professional action?",
 "Call the unit ahead of time", ["Arrive without calling", "Text a coworker to cover", "Stay late to make it up"],
 "Punctuality and reliability are part of professional behavior. If late, the technician calls ahead so the team can plan."),
("Which item is a medical error the technician must report?",
 "A tray given to the wrong patient", ["A patient asked for more water", "A patient refused a bath", "A call light answered late"],
 "Errors such as wrong-patient care are reported through the facility's process so they can be fixed and prevented, even if no harm resulted."),
]
